From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jon Pretty <jon.pretty@propensive.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 18:21:04 +0900
Subject: Do not suspend a unit read from TASTy for a macro with bytecode
 beside it

A unit which calls a macro defined in the same run is suspended and compiled
in a second run, because the macro's bytecode does not exist until the first
has finished. Whether a symbol is defined in the run is decided by whether its
file is one of the run's, and under `-from-tasty` those are the TASTy files
given: a macro among the inputs counted, and its callers were suspended,
though its class file lies beside its TASTy file and the macro runs from it.

The suspension is not only unnecessary there. A suspended unit read from
TASTy is not read again: the second run starts from the tree the first had
already transformed, so PostTyper adds the synthetic members of its case
classes and enums a second time, and the run fails with "Conflicting
definitions" as soon as another unit of the first run has referred to them.

A symbol read from a TASTy file with a class file beside it no longer counts
as awaiting its bytecode. One without is treated as before.

Written with LLM assistance (Claude Code); reviewed content.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Proscala-Patch: tastymacro
---
 .../src/dotty/tools/dotc/inlines/Inliner.scala | 18 ++++++++++++++++--
 .../from-tasty-macro-in-inputs/Macro_1.scala   |  7 +++++++
 .../from-tasty-macro-in-inputs/Other_2.scala   |  4 ++++
 .../from-tasty-macro-in-inputs/Use_2.scala     | 12 ++++++++++++
 4 files changed, 39 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)
 create mode 100644 tests/pos-macros/from-tasty-macro-in-inputs/Macro_1.scala
 create mode 100644 tests/pos-macros/from-tasty-macro-in-inputs/Other_2.scala
 create mode 100644 tests/pos-macros/from-tasty-macro-in-inputs/Use_2.scala

diff --git a/compiler/src/dotty/tools/dotc/inlines/Inliner.scala b/compiler/src/dotty/tools/dotc/inlines/Inliner.scala
index b7f568d9a2c5d6c923b83f41721f4498cfe0c608..3a9fce74b4e55ba517c32134021b90314bc7b940 100644
--- a/compiler/src/dotty/tools/dotc/inlines/Inliner.scala
+++ b/compiler/src/dotty/tools/dotc/inlines/Inliner.scala
@@ -1602,12 +1602,26 @@ class Inliner(val call: tpd.Tree)(using Context):
         tree match {
           case Closure(env, meth, tpt) if meth.symbol.isAnonymousFunction =>
             this(syms, tpt :: env)
-          case tree: RefTree if tree.isTerm && level == -1 && tree.symbol.isDefinedInCurrentRun && !tree.symbol.isLocal =>
+          case tree: RefTree if tree.isTerm && level == -1 && awaitsBytecode(tree.symbol) && !tree.symbol.isLocal =>
             foldOver(tree.symbol :: syms, tree)
-          case _: This if level == -1 && tree.symbol.isDefinedInCurrentRun =>
+          case _: This if level == -1 && awaitsBytecode(tree.symbol) =>
             tree.symbol :: syms
           case _: TypTree => syms
           case _ => foldOver(syms, tree)
         }
     }.apply(Nil, tree)
+
+  /** Is `sym` defined in the current run, with no bytecode to interpret it from yet?
+   *  A symbol compiled from source has none until its unit has been through the backend,
+   *  which is why a unit calling a macro defined in the same run is suspended. A symbol
+   *  read from a TASTy file given to `-from-tasty` is defined in the current run too,
+   *  but its class file lies beside the TASTy file, and the macro can be run from that.
+   */
+  private def awaitsBytecode(sym: Symbol)(using Context): Boolean =
+    sym.isDefinedInCurrentRun && !(ctx.settings.fromTasty.value && hasClassFile(sym))
+
+  private def hasClassFile(sym: Symbol)(using Context): Boolean =
+    val file = sym.associatedFile
+    file != null && file.ext.isTasty
+    && file.resolveSiblingWithExtension(dotty.tools.io.FileExtension.Class) != null
 end Inliner
